    "# Data Acquisition & Ingestion\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Data acquisition** tab in Edge Impulse is your central hub to collect, import, explore, and label the dataset used to train and validate your model.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because we are using an Arduino-compatible board streaming sensor data over USB, we will use the **Edge Impulse Data Forwarder** CLI tool to pipe readings straight from the board into the Edge Impulse cloud.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Connect Device via Data Forwarder\n",
    "\n",
    "The Data Forwarder captures comma-separated sensor readings from your board's serial output and transmits them to your Edge Impulse project.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 1: Upload Firmware and Free the Serial Port\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Upload your Arduino sketch to the board via the Arduino IDE.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Close the Arduino Serial Monitor and Arduino IDE.\n",
    "\n",
    ">Note: Only one program can bind to a serial COM port at a time. If the Arduino IDE or Serial Monitor keeps the port open, the CLI tool cannot establish a connection.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 2: Launch the Data Forwarder\n",
    "\n",
    "Open PowerShell (or your terminal) and run:\n",
    "`edge-impulse-data-forwarder`\n",
    "\n",
    "![cli_example.png](attachment:cli_example.png)\n",
    "_This is an example of the output of the forwarder._\n",
    "\n",
    "Follow the interactive prompts:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Log in: If prompted, enter your Edge Impulse credentials.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Select Project: Pick the project created for this workshop.\n",
    "![proj_sel.png](attachment:proj_sel.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "- If given the option, choose the port assigned to your microcontroller.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. Sensor Configuration\n",
    "![axes.png](attachment:axes.png)\n",
    "### Naming the Sensor Axes\n",
    "The forwarder inspects the serial stream and asks you to label each incoming variable:\n",
    "\n",
    "- How it maps to code: The number of axes corresponds directly to how many comma-separated values your Arduino code prints per line. \n",
    "\n",
    "For example: \n",
    "`(Serial.print(val1); Serial.print(\",\"); Serial.println(val2);)` will ask you to name 2 sensor axes.\n",
    "\n",
    "In our code:\n",
    "`Serial.println(buffer[i], 6);`\n",
    "\n",
    "This prints the value stored at index `i` of an array named `buffer` to the Serial Monitor with 6 decimal places of precision, and then moves the cursor to a new line. Since we are streaming a single current sensor value, we can label it "Current".\n",
    "\n",
    "### Name the Device\n",
    "\n",
    "Give the device an identifiable name (e.g., arduino-motor-setup). This helps identify where samples originated on the dashboard.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Auto-Detected Sampling Frequency\n",
    "\n",
    "The CLI tool reads incoming lines for a few seconds to estimate your sampling rate automatically:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Why it chooses a specific frequency: It calculates $\\frac{1}{\\Delta t}$ by measuring the average time delta between consecutive serial packet arrivals.\n",
    "- Tip: If your Arduino loop uses `delay(20);`, your stream generates roughly 50 packets per second, and the CLI will auto-detect approximately 50 Hz. \n",
    "- **Why the detected frequency fluctuates:** Even with identical Arduino code, you may see the detected frequency shift between runs. This jitter happens because:\n",
    "  - **Serial transmission latency & USB buffering:** The microcontroller, USB-to-UART bridge chip, OS drivers, and terminal buffer packets in chunks rather than streaming them with microsecond precision.\n",
    "  - **Non-blocking code overhead:** Blocking operations, sensor read delays (e.g., `analogRead()` execution time), or inconsistent loop iteration times cause slight timing variations.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3. Useful CLI Flags\n",
    "If you need to reconfigure or troubleshoot the connection:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Clear saved credentials/settings: `edge-impulse-data-forwarder --clean`\n",
    "- Explicitly lock the sampling frequency (avoids auto-detection drift): `edge-impulse-data-forwarder --frequency 50`\n",
    "- Override the baud rate (must match Serial.begin(...) in your sketch): `edge-impulse-data-forwarder --baud-rate 115200`\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4. Dataset Collection Best Practices\n",
    "When recording data from the Data Acquisition tab:\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Train / Test Split\n",
    "- Maintain roughly an 80/20 train/test split for every class (label) in your dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "![split.png](attachment:split.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "- Edge Impulse automatically tracks this ratio, but you can toggle between Training and Testing sets before sampling.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Collecting Robust Examples (e.g., Motor Stalls & Anomalies)\n",
    "ML models generalize poorly if all anomaly data looks identical. Capture variability in your dataset:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Varying stall durations:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src= \"Assets/stall1.png\" width = \"45%\"> <img src= \"Assets/stall3.png\" width = \"45%\">\n",
    "- Transient / micro-stalls: Quick disruptions or momentary jams (0.2–0.5 seconds).\n",
    "- Sustained stalls: Full lock-ups under continuous load (2–5 seconds).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Load variations:\n",
    "- Hard mechanical stops: Sudden lock with sharp current spikes.\n",
    "- Gradual friction jams: Drag increasing gradually until rotation stops.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. Recording Your First Samples\n",
    "\n",
    "![acq_data_tab.png](attachment:acq_data_tab.png)\n",
    "_Edge Impulse Data Acquisition tab after device is connected through Data Forwarder_\n",
    "\n",
    "Once the Data Forwarder is connected, return to the **Data acquisition** tab in your web browser.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Set the Label:**\n",
    "   - Type the class name for the data you are about to record.\n",
    "   - Examples: `idle` or `stall`.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Set Sample Length:**\n",
    "   - Set the duration in milliseconds (e.g., `10000` for a 10-second window).\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Start Sampling & Perform Action:**\n",
    "   - Click the **Start sampling** button.\n",
    "   - A short countdown will begin.\n",
    "   - **Actively produce the target behavior during the entire window:**\n",
    "     - If recording `idle`: Let the motor run unhindered or remain idle throughout the recording window.\n",
    "     - If recording `stall`: Introduce the mechanical stall during the active sampling period so the sensor captures the transient spike and sustained load.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Repeat Across Classes:**\n",
    "   - Collect multiple samples for each state. Ensure you record data under both the **Training** and **Testing** categories to maintain an 80/20 train/test split.\n",
    "\n",
    "![sample.png](attachment:sample.png)"
   ]
